    "case_summary": "A 34-year-old breastfeeding woman presented with a subacute painful, warm, and nodular skin lesion at the left side of her body localized between the neck and breast, measuring approximately 3 × 4 cm. One year earlier, the patient had visited an East-Turkish region and experienced a similar lesion that disappeared spontaneously after 10 days. She did not report consumption of unpasteurized dairy products or contact with livestock. Past Medical History: Born prematurely at 28 weeks' gestation with intracerebral bleeding and left hemiplegia, resulting in hydrocephaly, for which a VP shunt was placed. The VP shunt was completely revised at age 14 due to disconnection. Fourteen years later, a second revision was performed due to intracranial blockage, with a novel VP shunt placed at the right side of the thorax.\n\nNo systemic or other localized symptoms present. Vital signs:- Heart rate: 89 beats/min- Blood pressure: 124/73 mm Hg- Body temperature: 36.3 °C- Skin examination revealed a warm, nodular lesion measuring 3 × 4 cm between neck and breast\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Leukocyte count: 11.15 × 10⁹/L- C-reactive protein: 15 mg/L- Serological examination for antibodies: negativeImaging Studies:- Computed tomography of cranium without contrast: no abnormalities- Chest radiograph: no abnormalities- Ultrasound examination: showed inflammatory subcutaneous collection measuring 4 × 1.2 × 2 cm, diverging along median side of left VP shunt toward right VP shunt with diameter of 0.5 × 1 cm over length of 3-4 cmMicrobiological Findings:- Aspirate microscopy: showed amorphous debris and neutrophil granulocytes- Gram staining: showed Gram-negative coccobacilli- Culture: positive for B. melitensis biovar 3",
